n"></span><br></div><div class="token-line" style="color:#393A34"><span class="token plain">    </span><span class="token punctuation" style="color:#393A34">}</span><span class="token plain"></span><br></div><div class="token-line" style="color:#393A34"><span class="token plain" style="display:inline-block"></span><br></div><div class="token-line" style="color:#393A34"><span class="token plain">    </span><span class="token keyword" style="color:#00009f">async</span><span class="token plain"> </span><span class="token keyword" style="color:#00009f">fn</span><span class="token plain"> </span><span class="token function-definition function" style="color:#d73a49">allocate</span><span class="token punctuation" style="color:#393A34">(</span><span class="token operator" style="color:#393A34">&amp;</span><span class="token keyword" style="color:#00009f">self</span><span class="token punctuation" style="color:#393A34">,</span><span class="token plain"> len</span><span class="token punctuation" style="color:#393A34">:</span><span class="token plain"> </span><span class="token keyword" style="color:#00009f">usize</span><span class="token punctuation" style="color:#393A34">)</span><span class="token plain"> </span><span class="token punctuation" style="color:#393A34">-&gt;</span><span class="token plain"> </span><span class="token class-name">Option</span><span class="token operator" style="color:#393A34">&lt;</span><span class="token class-name">BudgetedBuffer</span><span class="token operator" style="color:#393A34">&gt;</span><span class="token plain"> </span><span class="token punctuation" style="color:#393A34">{</span><span class="token plain"></span><br></div><div class="token-line" style="color:#393A34"><span class="token plain">        </span><span class="token keyword" style="color:#00009f">if</span><span class="token plain"> len </span><span class="token operator" style="color:#393A34">&gt;</span><span class="token plain"> </span><span class="token keyword" style="color:#00009f">self</span><span class="token punctuation" style="color:#393A34">.</span><span class="token plain">capacity </span><span class="token punctuation" style="color:#393A34">{</span><span class="token plain"></span><br></div><div class="token-line" style="color:#393A34"><span class="token plain">            </span><span class="token keyword" style="color:#00009f">return</span><span class="token plain"> </span><span class="token class-name">None</span><span class="token punctuation" style="color:#393A34">;</span><span class="token plain"></span><br></div><div class="token-line" style="color:#393A34"><span class="token plain">        </span><span class="token punctuation" style="color:#393A34">}</span><span class="token plain"></span><br></div><div class="token-line" style="color:#393A34"><span class="token plain" style="display:inline-block"></span><br></div><div class="token-line" style="color:#393A34"><span class="token plain">        </span><span class="token keyword" style="color:#00009f">let</span><span class="token plain"> permit </span><span class="token operator" style="color:#393A34">=</span><span class="token plain"> </span><span class="token keyword" style="color:#00009f">self</span><span class="token punctuation" style="color:#393A34">.</span><span class="token plain">available</span><span class="token punctuation" style="color:#393A34">.</span><span class="token function" style="color:#d73a49">clone</span><span class="token punctuation" style="color:#393A34">(</span><span class="token punctuation" style="color:#393A34">)</span><span class="token punctuation" style="color:#393A34">.</span><span class="token function" style="color:#d73a49">acquire_owned</span><span class="token punctuation" style="color:#393A34">(</span><span class="token plain">len</span><span class="token punctuation" style="color:#393A34">)</span><span class="token punctuation" style="color:#393A34">.</span><span class="token keyword" style="color:#00009f">await</span><span class="token punctuation" style="color:#393A34">;</span><span class="token plain"></span><br></div><div class="token-line" style="color:#393A34"><span class="token plain">        </span><span class="token class-name">Some</span><span class="token punctuation" style="color:#393A34">(</span><span class="token class-name">BudgetedBuffer</span><span class="token plain"> </span><span class="token punctuation" style="color:#393A34">{</span><span class="token plain"></span><br></div><div class="token-line" style="color:#393A34"><span class="token plain">            data</span><span class="token punctuation" style="color:#393A34">:</span><span class="token plain"> </span><span class="token macro property" style="color:#36acaa">vec!</span><span class="token punctuation" style="color:#393A34">[</span><span class="token number" style="color:#36acaa">0</span><span class="token punctuation" style="color:#393A34">;</span><span class="token plain"> len</span><span class="token punctuation" style="color:#393A34">]</span><span class="token punctuation" style="color:#393A34">,</span><span class="token plain"></span><br></div><div class="token-line" style="color:#393A34"><span class="token plain">            _permit</span><span class="token punctuation" style="color:#393A34">:</span><span class="token plain"> permit</span><span class="token punctuation" style="color:#393A34">,</span><span class="token plain"></span><br></div><div class="token-line" style="color:#393A34"><span class="token plain">        </span><span class="token punctuation" style="color:#393A34">}</span><span class="token punctuation" style="color:#393A34">)</span><span class="token plain"></span><br></div><div class="token-line" style="color:#393A34"><span class="token plain">    </span><span class="token punctuation" style="color:#393A34">}</span><span class="token plain"></span><br></div><div class="token-line" style="color:#393A34"><span class="token plain"></span><span class="token punctuation" style="color:#393A34">}</span><br></div></code></pre></div></div>
<p>With <code>BufferBudget::new(8 * 1024 * 1024)</code>, the budget covers eight MiB of payload. A 64 KiB allocation charges 64 KiB; a two MiB allocation charges two MiB. The buffer and its reservation can move together between tasks. Dropping the buffer frees its data and returns the permits.</p>
<p>Rejecting a request larger than the entire fixed budget is necessary: returning every outstanding permit could still never satisfy it. Reserve the full amount in one acquisition, too. Acquiring a large reservation incrementally can leave several callers each holding some capacity while all wait for the rest.</p>
<p>This budget counts the payload bytes in these buffers. Allocator overhead, other allocations, and extra copies require their own accounting. Growing or copying a buffer must reserve the additional capacity before doing so.</p>
<h2 class="anchor anchorTargetStickyNavbar_zq6S" id="decide-what-happens-when-capacity-runs-out">Decide what happens when capacity runs out<a href="https://asyncband.apache.org/blog/2026/10/14/choosing-the-right-primitive/#decide-what-happens-when-capacity-runs-out" class="hash-link" aria-label="Direct link to Decide what happens when capacity runs out" title="Direct link to Decide what happens when capacity runs out" translate="no">​</a></h2>
<p>Waiting is one admission policy. <code>acquire(n).await</code> waits for capacity; <code>try_acquire(n)</code> returns <code>None</code> when it cannot acquire the requested permits immediately. A caller can use the latter to reject or defer work instead of joining the wait queue. A deadline around <code>acquire</code> gives waiting a time limit.</p>
<p>The placement of the acquisition matters. If a program allocates a large payload and only then waits for its permit, that payload already consumes memory outside the budget. Likewise, spawning one task per incoming job and acquiring inside each task limits admitted work while allowing an arbitrarily large population of waiting tasks. Apply admission before creating the resource whose growth you intend to bound, or bound the waiting population separately.</p>
<p>Asyncband assigns permits to queued acquisitions in order. A large acquisition at the front can therefore hold back smaller ones behind it. If a budget has three units left and the first waiter needs five, a later one-unit waiter waits too. This preserves ordering, with a latency cost when request sizes vary substantially.</p>
<p>Dropping a pending acquisition removes it from the queue without leaking permits; retrying joins at the back. After acquisition completes, dropping the permit guard returns its permits. This accounting does not undo application side effects or stop work that has been handed to another task; the task doing that work must retain the permit for its actual lifetime.</p>
<h2 class="anchor anchorTargetStickyNavbar_zq6S" id="adjust-a-running-budget">Adjust a running budget<a href="https://asyncband.apache.org/blog/2026/10/14/choosing-the-right-primitive/#adjust-a-running-budget" class="hash-link" aria-label="Direct link to Adjust a running budget" title="Direct link to Adjust a running budget" translate="no">​</a></h2>
<p>Some applications change their concurrency budget while work is already running. Asyncband provides two operations for this: <code>release(n)</code> adds capacity, and <a href="https://docs.rs/asyncband/0.7.3/asyncband/semaphore/struct.Semaphore.html#method.reduce_permits" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class=""><code>reduce_permits(n)</code></a> removes exactly <code>n</code> permits from the logical balance. A reduction does not revoke permits that callers already hold. If insufficient permits are currently available, later returns repay the deficit before ordinary queued acquisitions proceed.</p>
<p>For example, suppose an eight-slot budget is fully occupied and its controller reduces it by three. The eight operations can finish normally. As their guards are dropped, three returned permits retire the deficit; after all eight return, five slots remain available. The controller should serialize budget changes so that each adjustment is applied once.</p>
<p><a href="https://docs.rs/asyncband/0.7.3/asyncband/semaphore/struct.Semaphore.html#method.drain_permits" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class=""><code>drain_permits(n)</code></a> only removes permits that are available at the time and returns the number removed. Draining three from that fully occupied budget removes zero, so it cannot promise the same capacity reduction. Also, returning an ordinary guard already restores its permits: an additional <code>release</code> would add capacity a second time.</p>
<p>For a fixed limit, acquiring and dropping guards is enough. Add weighted reservations or capacity adjustments when the resource being controlled calls for them, and keep the permit's lifetime aligned with the work it accounts for.</p>]]></content>
